\title{Ten hexagons through every edge: three exterior edges}
\author{Human Compression project}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle

\begin{theorem}\label{main}
A finite simple quartic graph of girth six with exactly ten hexagons
through every edge has at least thirty-nine vertices.
\end{theorem}
This manuscript addresses the lower bound in the $\lambda=10$ row of
Table~2, physical page~18, of Goedgebeur--Jooken, \emph{Exhaustive generation
of edge-girth-regular graphs}, arXiv:2401.08271v2, 25 June 2024.
The complete finite record specified below is part of the proof. No
existence or uniqueness assertion at order thirty-nine is made.

\section{The exterior consists of four possible forests}
Fix an edge $uv$. Its two rooted trees of depth two have twenty-six
vertices: each side has three branches, with three leaves in each branch.
Girth six permits additional edges in this vertex set only between
opposite leaves. Between any two opposite branches these edges form a
matching, and each edge completes exactly one hexagon through $uv$.
There are therefore ten such edges. If the nine matching sizes are
$3-d_{ij}$, then
\[
 0\le d_{ij}\le3,\qquad\sum_{i,j}d_{ij}=17.                 \tag{1}
\]
There are seventeen missing leaf incidences on each side. With $Y$ the
set of remaining vertices and $t=|Y|$, the degree sum gives
\[
                  2e(Y)=4t-34.                            \tag{2}
\]
Thus $n\ge35$. Counting hexagons by their edges gives $10n/3$ hexagons,
so $3\mid n$. To prove the theorem it suffices to exclude $n=36$.
At this order $|Y|=10$ and $e(Y)=3$. Since there is no triangle, the
induced graph on $Y$ is one of
\[
 3K_2+4K_1,\quad P_3+K_2+5K_1,\quad
 P_4+6K_1,\quad K_{1,3}+6K_1.                              \tag{3}
\]

Write $h_i$ for the degree of $i\in Y$ in this forest, and put $k_i=4-h_i$.
Let $a_i$ be its number of left leaf neighbours. Its right degree is
$k_i-a_i$, and
\[
 \max(0,k_i-3)\le a_i\le\min(3,k_i),\qquad\sum_i a_i=17.   \tag{4}
\]
The upper bound three on either side follows because two neighbours in
one branch would give a quadrangle. If $ij$ is an exterior edge, their
neighbours cannot lie in the same rooted branch: equality of neighbours
gives a triangle and distinct neighbours give a pentagon. Consequently
\[
 a_i+a_j\le3,\qquad (k_i-a_i)+(k_j-a_j)\le3.               \tag{5}
\]
Vertices at exterior distance two or three may meet the same branch at
different leaves; no such exclusion is imposed.

Let $P_L,P_R$ be the nine missing leaf degrees on the two sides. The
acquired root equations give the exterior pair totals
\[
 F_L=f(P_R),\quad F_R=f(P_L),\qquad
 f(P)=\frac{51-\sum_{s\in P}s^2}{2}.                       \tag{6}
\]
Indeed the four edges at $u$ count every hexagon through $u$ twice, so ten
hexagons through $u$ avoid $uv$. An old opposite leaf of missing degree
$s$ contributes $\binom{3-s}{2}$ pairs of neighbours in different left
branches. Subtracting these contributions gives (6), and the right-side
argument is identical. An exterior edge cannot enter this four-edge
path between two branches. The three individual branch-pair equations
are also retained, not merely their sums.

Subtracting the pair sums supplied by the vertices of $Y$ yields
\[
 F_L-F_R=12-\tfrac12\sum_i h_i^2-\sum_i h_i a_i.            \tag{7}
\]
Every edge contributes $a_i+a_j$ to the last sum. From (5), the absolute
difference is at most $0,1,2,3$ for the four forests in (3), respectively.
For example an isolated edge has $a_i+a_j=3$; an end edge of $P_4$ has
sum two or three, and its middle edge has sum one, two or three.
The finite degree inventory enumerates (4)--(5) for each literal forest;
it has $1216,2136,3738,5814$ vectors. This is a necessary inventory,
not a claim that those degree vectors are realizable.

\section{Normalization and complete boundary construction}
We acquire the proved matching-forest normalization for (1). Permuting
branches and interchanging the two sides gives the complete deficit
matrix inventory. Along a forest of positive matching blocks, successive
permutations of the three leaves normalize each child matching; all
remaining matching choices are visited. A proposed identification of two
root graphs is accepted only after checking its literal bijection against
every adjacency and the root coloring. The isomorphism program proposes
maps; an unverified proposed map cannot remove a root.

For the boundary, fix one forest $H$ from (3). For each of its labelled
vertices choose subsets $B_i,C_i$ of the nine left and nine right leaves.
These are precisely its remaining neighbours, with
$|B_i|+|C_i|=k_i$. Each subset meets a branch at most once, and each leaf
must occur its prescribed missing number of times. All edges of a graph
of order thirty-six are now specified.

\begin{lemma}\label{normal}
The following restrictions on $B_i$ preserve every isomorphism class of
completions. No further ordering of the $C_i$ is required or imposed.
\begin{enumerate}
\item For $3K_2$, order the two masks on every edge, then order the three
pairs lexicographically.
\item For $P_3+K_2$, order the two path-end masks and order the two masks
on the separate edge.
\item For $P_4$, require its mask sequence to be no greater than its reverse.
\item For $K_{1,3}$, order the three leaf masks.
\end{enumerate}
In each case order the masks on the isolated vertices. Equal masks are allowed.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Use the integer encoding of subsets as a fixed total order. The listed
operations generate the automorphism group of each nontrivial forest
component; equal components can be permuted, as can all isolated vertices.
Apply these actual forest automorphisms to any completed graph until the
listed inequalities hold. They also transport its right subsets $C_i$.
Every resulting right subset remains among the unrestricted choices.
In particular ties between left masks justify no additional restriction
on the right. The comparisons applied during recursion involve only
already assigned masks, so every normalized complete assignment satisfies
them at every earlier stage as well.
\end{proof}

The program chooses $(B_i,C_i)$ together, in a fixed exterior-vertex order.
This is a different ordering of the same choices as selecting all $B_i$
first and then all $C_i$. For a suffix of the vertex order it precomputes
all triples
\[
 \left(\sum a_i,\;\sum\binom{a_i}{2},\;
                    \sum\binom{k_i-a_i}{2}\right)          \tag{8}
\]
permitted by the individual bounds (4). At a partial graph, every
completion supplies the remaining left incidence sum and the two
remaining pair totals. Absence of that triple from (8) proves impossibility.
Presence does not prove existence: this inventory deliberately ignores
geometry and the three individual pair equations.

Every other rejection is also necessary. Leaf capacities may not become
negative. The individual pair totals are sums of nonnegative contributions,
so a partial sum may not exceed its prescribed total. Before an edge is
inserted, its endpoints must have distance at least five in the current
graph. A shorter path would persist and create a prohibited short cycle.
This criterion is valid whether the other side of the same exterior
vertex has already been assigned. At termination all degrees are four,
the graph has girth at least six, and there are no unknown edges. For each
edge, the number of simple paths of length five between its endpoints is
exactly its number of hexagons. Testing that number against ten finishes
the exact graph test.

\section{The finite record and its scope}
The root traversal includes all $18351$ deficit matrices, $310$ matrix
orbits and $3565944$ normalized matching products. It visits $3094065$
girth-admissible roots, checks $960936$ literal maps after the profile
filter, and retains $15799$ representatives. Every representative is
passed to each of the four boundary forests. The final complete boundary
record is required here; an incomplete call supplies no exclusion.

\input{boundary_table.tex}

Independent finite controls compare all $236196$ three-colour assignments
on the four exterior forests with their actual automorphism orbits. The
implemented inequalities select exactly one representative in each of
$5208$ orbits. Cartesian enumeration independently checks $1011607$
suffix degree choices. A known positive graph on thirty-two vertices with
$\lambda=18$ is recovered with a checked literal map. On twelve relabellings
of its root and all four forests, the separate and joint traversals agree
on complete counts and every positive adjacency array. These controls
supplement the coverage proof; they do not replace the target calculation.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{main}]
Equations (1)--(2) and hexagon divisibility reduce the assertion to order
thirty-six. The normalized matching traversal covers every possible root
configuration, with literal verification of every reuse. Equations (3)--(6),
Lemma~\ref{normal} and the boundary argument cover every possible completion.
The required complete finite record excludes each one. Thus order
thirty-six is impossible and the asserted lower bound follows.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Attribution.}
The lower bound belongs to the fixed source row. The Moore configuration,
root pair equations, matching-forest normalization and literal map method
are acquired from the reviewed preceding components, including the
twelve-hexagon component. The new work is the three-edge exterior analysis,
the exact forest normalization of left subsets, and the joint boundary
constructor with its coverage argument and complete record. A separate
collaborator gave a preliminary read of the already written analytic
argument and implementation; the final distinct review is recorded
separately. The first separate boundary attempt reached its deadline and
is preserved without an exclusion claim. No priority, open-conjecture,
whole-source, Lean or compression-factor credit is asserted.
